<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 1: </span>Discriminative models for (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>) deterministic and (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>) binary-stochastic forward-forward.
 In Hinton’s FF, <math id="S3.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to labeled images and <math id="S3.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> to indicates whether the label is good or bad; in CwC-FF, <math id="S3.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the image and <math id="S3.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> the one-hot label.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Logistic Forward-Forward</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Before deriving binary stochastic forward-forward algorithms, we begin with the real-valued, deterministic version, in particular two-layer neural networks with logistic (sigmoidal) units <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In order to cover both Bernoulli and categorical random variables, we write the output distribution as a generic exponential family (suppressing all biases for concision),</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}a_{d}&amp;=\sigma\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\bm{w}^{d}_{\text{in}}\cdot\bm{v}}}\right),\qquad d\in[1,{D}]\\
\hat{p}\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{z}}{}|{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{v}}{};\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{in}},\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{out}}}}\right)&amp;=h({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{z}})\exp\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left\{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{z}}^{\text{T}}\bm{\eta}{}(\bm{a})-A\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\bm{\eta}{}(\bm{a})}}\right)}}\right\},\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mtext>in</mtext><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>𝒗</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="4.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒛</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒗</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒛</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>exp</mi><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒛</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mtext>T</mtext></msup><mi>𝜼</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>𝜼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}a_{d}&amp;=\sigma\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\bm{w}^{d}_{\text{in}}\cdot\bm{v}}}\right),\qquad d\in[1,{D}]\\
\hat{p}\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{z}}{}|{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{v}}{};\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{in}},\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{out}}}}\right)&amp;=h({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{z}})\exp\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left\{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{z}}^{\text{T}}\bm{\eta}{}(\bm{a})-A\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\bm{\eta}{}(\bm{a})}}\right)}}\right\},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{w}^{d}_{\text{in}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mtext>in</mtext><mi>d</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{w}^{d}_{\text{in}}</annotation></semantics></math> is row <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{in}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{in}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the natural parameters <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\eta}{}\mathrel{\vbox{\hbox{\scriptsize.}\hbox{\scriptsize.}}}=\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{out}}\bm{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜼</mi><mtext>
<span class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_bottom"> 
<span class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">.</span></span> 
<span class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">.</span></span> 
</span></mtext><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒂</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\eta}{}\mathrel{\vbox{\hbox{\scriptsize.}\hbox{\scriptsize.}}}=\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{out}}\bm{a}</annotation></semantics></math> are linear in the hidden-layer activities, and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\cdot}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\cdot}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is the logistic (sigmoid) function.
Our objective is to minimize the (conditional) relative entropy of the data and model:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrel{\vbox{\hbox{\scriptsize.}\hbox{\scriptsize.}}}=\KL\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left\{p\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left({\bm{Z}}|{\bm{V}}}}\right)\middle\|\hat{p}\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left({\bm{Z}}|{\bm{V}};\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{in}},\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{out}}}}\right)}}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>
<span class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_bottom"> 
<span class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">.</span></span> 
<span class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">.</span></span> 
</span></mtext><mo lspace="0em">=</mo><msub><mtext>D</mtext><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>p</mi><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrel{\vbox{\hbox{\scriptsize.}\hbox{\scriptsize.}}}=\KL\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left\{p\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left({\bm{Z}}|{\bm{V}}}}\right)\middle\|\hat{p}\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left({\bm{Z}}|{\bm{V}};\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{in}},\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{out}}}}\right)}}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle={\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left\langle{A\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\bm{\eta}{}(\bm{a})}}\right)-{\bm{Z}}^{\text{T}}\bm{\eta}{}(\bm{a})}}}\right\rangle_{{\bm{Z}}{},{\bm{V}}{}}}+C," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mi>A</mi><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>𝜼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒁</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mtext>T</mtext></msup><mi>𝜼</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>𝒁</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑽</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle={\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left\langle{A\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\bm{\eta}{}(\bm{a})}}\right)-{\bm{Z}}^{\text{T}}\bm{\eta}{}(\bm{a})}}}\right\rangle_{{\bm{Z}}{},{\bm{V}}{}}}+C,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> a constant independent of the parameters.
The gradient of this loss with respect to <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{w}^{l}_{\text{in}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mtext>in</mtext><mi>l</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{w}^{l}_{\text{in}}</annotation></semantics></math>, a single row of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{in}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{in}}</annotation></semantics></math>, takes on the (familiar) form</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\frac{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}{\mathcal{L}}}{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}{\bm{w}^{l}_{\text{in}}}}&amp;={\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left\langle{\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\frac{\partial{A}}{\partial{\bm{\eta}{}}}(\bm{\eta}{}(\bm{a}))-{\bm{Z}}}}\right)^{\text{T}}\frac{\partial{\bm{\eta}{}}}{\partial{\bm{a}}}\frac{\partial{\bm{a}}}{\partial{\bm{w}^{l}_{\text{in}}}}}}}\right\rangle_{{\bm{Z}}{},{\bm{V}}{}}}\\
&amp;={\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left\langle{\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\psi\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{out}}\bm{a}}}\right)-{\bm{Z}}}}\right)^{\text{T}}\bm{w}^{l}_{\text{out}}a_{l}(1-a_{l}){\bm{V}}}}}\right\rangle_{{\bm{Z}}{},{\bm{V}}{}}},\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mfrac><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mtext>in</mtext><mi>l</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝜼</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝜼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒁</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mtext>T</mtext></msup><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝜼</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝒂</mi></mrow></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝒂</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mtext>in</mtext><mi>l</mi></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>⟩</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝒁</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑽</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒂</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒁</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mtext>T</mtext></msup><mi>𝒘</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>𝑽</mi></mrow><mo>⟩</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝒁</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑽</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\frac{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}{\mathcal{L}}}{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}{\bm{w}^{l}_{\text{in}}}}&amp;={\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left\langle{\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\frac{\partial{A}}{\partial{\bm{\eta}{}}}(\bm{\eta}{}(\bm{a}))-{\bm{Z}}}}\right)^{\text{T}}\frac{\partial{\bm{\eta}{}}}{\partial{\bm{a}}}\frac{\partial{\bm{a}}}{\partial{\bm{w}^{l}_{\text{in}}}}}}}\right\rangle_{{\bm{Z}}{},{\bm{V}}{}}}\\
&amp;={\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left\langle{\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\psi\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{out}}\bm{a}}}\right)-{\bm{Z}}}}\right)^{\text{T}}\bm{w}^{l}_{\text{out}}a_{l}(1-a_{l}){\bm{V}}}}}\right\rangle_{{\bm{Z}}{},{\bm{V}}{}}},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{w}^{l}_{\text{out}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mtext>out</mtext><mi>l</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{w}^{l}_{\text{out}}</annotation></semantics></math> the <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l^{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>l</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l^{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> column of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{out}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{out}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
On the second line we have used the fact that, for exponential families, the gradient of the log-partition function is the expectation of the sufficient statistics, in this case <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\hat{Z}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒁</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\hat{Z}}}</annotation></semantics></math>; or, equivalently, the inverse link (logistic for Bernoulli outputs, the softmax for categorical, etc.), which we refer to here as <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\cdot}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\cdot}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, applied to the natural parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Ordinarily, (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In III-A Logistic Forward-Forward ‣ III Theory ‣ Energy-Efficient Supervised Learning with aBinary Stochastic Forward-Forward Algorithm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) is interpreted as propagating the output gradient,
<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{out}}\bm{a}}}\right)-{\bm{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒂</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒁</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{out}}\bm{a}}}\right)-{\bm{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
backwards through the output weights <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{w}^{l}_{\text{out}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mtext>out</mtext><mi>l</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{w}^{l}_{\text{out}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which raises questions for biological plausibility (how could the synaptic weights in the feedback path be locked to the feedforward weights?) and hardware implementations (see Section <a href="#S1" title="I Introduction ‣ Energy-Efficient Supervised Learning with aBinary Stochastic Forward-Forward Algorithm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>).
In the forward-forward algorithm, however, the elements of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{out}}{}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{out}}{}</annotation></semantics></math> are fixed and equal (or zero; see (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In III Theory ‣ Energy-Efficient Supervised Learning with aBinary Stochastic Forward-Forward Algorithm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In III Theory ‣ Energy-Efficient Supervised Learning with aBinary Stochastic Forward-Forward Algorithm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>)), so <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{w}^{l}_{\text{out}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mtext>out</mtext><mi>l</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{w}^{l}_{\text{out}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be absorbed into the gradient step size for all <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Binary Stochastic Forward-Forward</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To port this algorithm efficiently to hardware, we need to replace the matrix multiplications with indexing and adding.
This can be achieved if we make the hidden units binary- rather than real-valued.
Simply thresholding the outputs, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, of the first layer in (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In III-A Logistic Forward-Forward ‣ III Theory ‣ Energy-Efficient Supervised Learning with aBinary Stochastic Forward-Forward Algorithm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) discards useful information; it is preferable to draw samples, which across multiple training inputs provides real-valued information.
Conveniently, drawing Bernoulli samples is exceedingly cheap under the proposed hardware platform (Section <a href="#S2.SS3" title="II-C Probabilistic Bits ‣ II Background ‣ Energy-Efficient Supervised Learning with aBinary Stochastic Forward-Forward Algorithm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II-C</span></a>).
Therefore, let the real values <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}</annotation></semantics></math> specify the firing probabilities, rather than the activities, of random hidden units <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\hat{U}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑼</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\hat{U}}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\hat{p}\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{u}}{}|{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{v}}{};\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{in}}}}\right)&amp;=\prod_{d=1}^{{D}}\hat{p}\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}u_{d}}|{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{v}}{};w^{d}_{\text{in}}}}\right)\\
&amp;=\prod_{d=1}^{{D}}\sigma\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\bm{w}^{d}_{\text{in}}\cdot{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{v}}}}\right)^{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}u_{d}}}\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(1-\sigma\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\bm{w}^{d}_{\text{in}}\cdot{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{v}}}}\right)}}\right)^{1-{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}u_{d}}}\\
\hat{p}\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{z}}{}|{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{u}}{};\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{out}}}}\right)&amp;=h({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{z}})\exp\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left\{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{z}}^{\text{T}}\bm{\eta}{}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{u}})-A\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\bm{\eta}{}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{u}})}}\right)}}\right\},\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒖</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒗</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>D</mi></munderover><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒗</mi><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mtext>in</mtext><mi>d</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msup><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>D</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mtext>in</mtext><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒗</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mtext>in</mtext><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒗</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒛</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒖</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒛</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>exp</mi><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒛</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mtext>T</mtext></msup><mi>𝜼</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>𝜼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\hat{p}\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{u}}{}|{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{v}}{};\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{in}}}}\right)&amp;=\prod_{d=1}^{{D}}\hat{p}\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}u_{d}}|{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{v}}{};w^{d}_{\text{in}}}}\right)\\
&amp;=\prod_{d=1}^{{D}}\sigma\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\bm{w}^{d}_{\text{in}}\cdot{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{v}}}}\right)^{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}u_{d}}}\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(1-\sigma\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\bm{w}^{d}_{\text{in}}\cdot{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{v}}}}\right)}}\right)^{1-{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}u_{d}}}\\
\hat{p}\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{z}}{}|{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{u}}{};\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{out}}}}\right)&amp;=h({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{z}})\exp\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left\{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{z}}^{\text{T}}\bm{\eta}{}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{u}})-A\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\bm{\eta}{}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{u}})}}\right)}}\right\},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with the natural parameters now linear in the binary samples, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\eta}{}=\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{out}}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜼</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒖</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\eta}{}=\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{out}}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\bm{u}}</annotation></semantics></math>, rather than <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{a}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To fit this model, we ought to minimize the same relative entropy as in the deterministic case, (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In III-A Logistic Forward-Forward ‣ III Theory ‣ Energy-Efficient Supervised Learning with aBinary Stochastic Forward-Forward Algorithm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>)—but in the stochastic case, this equation hides the fact that the latent Bernoulli random variables <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\hat{U}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑼</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\hat{U}}}</annotation></semantics></math> have been marginalized out.
The gradient likewise takes a superficially simple form, but in practice introduces an intractable normalizer.
Although this can be finessed with an importance sampler (see Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Loss Functions for Binary Stochastic Forward-Forward ‣ Energy-Efficient Supervised Learning with aBinary Stochastic Forward-Forward Algorithm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>), we achieve superior results by instead descending the gradient of a “variational” upper bound.
In particular, we consider the loss in (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In III-A Logistic Forward-Forward ‣ III Theory ‣ Energy-Efficient Supervised Learning with aBinary Stochastic Forward-Forward Algorithm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) to be a “marginal” relative entropy, and then follow the classic variational approach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> in constructing the following “joint” relative entropy (suppressing parameters for brevity):</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathcal{J}&amp;\mathrel{\vbox{\hbox{\scriptsize.}\hbox{\scriptsize.}}}=\KL\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left\{p\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left({\bm{Z}}|{\bm{V}}}}\right)\hat{p}\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left({\bm{\hat{U}}}|{\bm{V}}}}\right)\middle\|\hat{p}\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left({\bm{Z}},{\bm{\hat{U}}}|{\bm{V}}}}\right)}}\right\}\\
&amp;={\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left\langle{\sum_{\bm{\hat{u}}{}}\hat{p}\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\bm{\hat{u}}|{\bm{V}}}}\right)\log\hat{p}\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left({\bm{Z}}|\bm{\hat{u}}}}\right)}}}\right\rangle_{{\bm{Z}}{},{\bm{V}}{}}}+C.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>
<span class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_bottom"> 
<span class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">.</span></span> 
<span class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">.</span></span> 
</span></mtext><mo lspace="0em">=</mo><msub><mtext>D</mtext><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>p</mi><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝑽</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">)</mo><mi>log</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝒁</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⟩</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝒁</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑽</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathcal{J}&amp;\mathrel{\vbox{\hbox{\scriptsize.}\hbox{\scriptsize.}}}=\KL\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left\{p\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left({\bm{Z}}|{\bm{V}}}}\right)\hat{p}\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left({\bm{\hat{U}}}|{\bm{V}}}}\right)\middle\|\hat{p}\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left({\bm{Z}},{\bm{\hat{U}}}|{\bm{V}}}}\right)}}\right\}\\
&amp;={\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left\langle{\sum_{\bm{\hat{u}}{}}\hat{p}\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\bm{\hat{u}}|{\bm{V}}}}\right)\log\hat{p}\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left({\bm{Z}}|\bm{\hat{u}}}}\right)}}}\right\rangle_{{\bm{Z}}{},{\bm{V}}{}}}+C.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This is an upper bound on the relative entropy in (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In III-A Logistic Forward-Forward ‣ III Theory ‣ Energy-Efficient Supervised Learning with aBinary Stochastic Forward-Forward Algorithm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) because it can be expressed as the sum of that relative entropy and a non-negative quantity (another relative entropy):</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{J}=\KL\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left\{p\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left({\bm{Z}}|{\bm{V}}}}\right)\middle\|\hat{p}\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left({\bm{Z}}|{\bm{V}}}}\right)}}\right\}+\KL\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left\{\hat{p}\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left({\bm{\hat{U}}}|{\bm{V}}}}\right)\middle\|\hat{p}\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left({\bm{\hat{U}}}|{\bm{Z}},{\bm{V}}}}\right)}}\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mtext>D</mtext><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>p</mi><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mtext>D</mtext><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>{</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐕</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}=\KL\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left\{p\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left({\bm{Z}}|{\bm{V}}}}\right)\middle\|\hat{p}\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left({\bm{Z}}|{\bm{V}}}}\right)}}\right\}+\KL\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left\{\hat{p}\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left({\bm{\hat{U}}}|{\bm{V}}}}\right)\middle\|\hat{p}\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left({\bm{\hat{U}}}|{\bm{Z}},{\bm{V}}}}\right)}}\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">(See Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Loss Functions for Binary Stochastic Forward-Forward ‣ Energy-Efficient Supervised Learning with aBinary Stochastic Forward-Forward Algorithm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for a fuller derivation.)
We also observe that the upper bound can be expected to be fairly tight: The input <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> should provide many more bits of information about <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\hat{U}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑼</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\hat{U}}}</annotation></semantics></math> than <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which provides only <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log{{K}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log{{K}}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{K}</annotation></semantics></math> the number of categories.
In the case of Hinton’s forward-forward algorithm, this is just one bit.
So the second relative entropy should be small.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The gradient of this loss (in particular, the last line of (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In III-B Binary Stochastic Forward-Forward ‣ III Theory ‣ Energy-Efficient Supervised Learning with aBinary Stochastic Forward-Forward Algorithm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>)) is tractable, and can be written (see Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Loss Functions for Binary Stochastic Forward-Forward ‣ Energy-Efficient Supervised Learning with aBinary Stochastic Forward-Forward Algorithm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>) as</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\frac{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}{\mathcal{J}}}{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}{\bm{w}^{l}_{\text{in}}}}&amp;\approx{\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left\langle{\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{\hat{U}}}{}|{\bm{V}}}{\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left[\psi\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{out}}{\bm{\hat{U}}}}}\right)\middle|{\bm{V}}{}}}\right]}-{\bm{Z}}}}\right)^{\text{T}}\bm{w}^{l}_{\text{out}}a_{l}(1-a_{l}){\bm{V}}}}}\right\rangle_{{\bm{Z}}{},{\bm{V}}{}}}.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mfrac><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mtext>in</mtext><mi>l</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>≈</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑼</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝑽</mi></mrow></msub><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑼</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>𝑽</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒁</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mtext>T</mtext></msup><mi>𝒘</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>𝑽</mi></mrow><mo>⟩</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝒁</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑽</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\frac{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}{\mathcal{J}}}{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}{\bm{w}^{l}_{\text{in}}}}&amp;\approx{\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left\langle{\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{\hat{U}}}{}|{\bm{V}}}{\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left[\psi\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{out}}{\bm{\hat{U}}}}}\right)\middle|{\bm{V}}{}}}\right]}-{\bm{Z}}}}\right)^{\text{T}}\bm{w}^{l}_{\text{out}}a_{l}(1-a_{l}){\bm{V}}}}}\right\rangle_{{\bm{Z}}{},{\bm{V}}{}}}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Note the general resemblance between (<a href="#S3.E10" title="In III-B Binary Stochastic Forward-Forward ‣ III Theory ‣ Energy-Efficient Supervised Learning with aBinary Stochastic Forward-Forward Algorithm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In III-A Logistic Forward-Forward ‣ III Theory ‣ Energy-Efficient Supervised Learning with aBinary Stochastic Forward-Forward Algorithm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).
Thus, the only change in the learning rule required by the move from the deterministic to the stochastic network is to replace the model class probabilities, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{out}}\bm{a}}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒂</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{out}}\bm{a}}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, with the <em id="S3.SS2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">expected</em> class probabilities,
<math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{\hat{U}}}{}|{\bm{V}}}{\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left[\psi\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{out}}{\bm{\hat{U}}}}}\right)\middle|{\bm{V}}{}}}\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑼</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝑽</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑼</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>𝑽</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{\hat{U}}}{}|{\bm{V}}}{\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left[\psi\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{out}}{\bm{\hat{U}}}}}\right)\middle|{\bm{V}}{}}}\right]}</annotation></semantics></math>—although we recall that (<a href="#S3.E10" title="In III-B Binary Stochastic Forward-Forward ‣ III Theory ‣ Energy-Efficient Supervised Learning with aBinary Stochastic Forward-Forward Algorithm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) is the gradient of an upper bound to the loss, rather than the loss itself.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Methods</span></h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Approximating the BSFF gradient</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, we approximate the conditional expectation in (<a href="#S3.E10" title="In III-B Binary Stochastic Forward-Forward ‣ III Theory ‣ Energy-Efficient Supervised Learning with aBinary Stochastic Forward-Forward Algorithm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) with a single sample vector, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\hat{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\hat{u}}</annotation></semantics></math>, from the hidden layer for each input/output pair (<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{v},\bm{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒛</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{v},\bm{z}</annotation></semantics></math>); hence</p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\frac{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}{\mathcal{J}}}{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}{\bm{w}^{l}_{\text{in}}}}\approx{\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left\langle{\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\psi\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{out}}{\bm{\hat{U}}}}}\right)-{\bm{Z}}}}\right)^{\text{T}}\bm{w}^{l}_{\text{out}}a_{l}(1-a_{l}){\bm{V}}}}}\right\rangle_{{\bm{Z}}{},{\bm{\hat{U}}}{},{\bm{V}}{}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mtext>in</mtext><mi>l</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑼</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒁</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mtext>T</mtext></msup><mi>𝒘</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>𝑽</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>𝒁</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑼</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑽</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}{\mathcal{J}}}{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}{\bm{w}^{l}_{\text{in}}}}\approx{\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left\langle{\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\psi\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left(\mathbf{{W}}_{\text{out}}{\bm{\hat{U}}}}}\right)-{\bm{Z}}}}\right)^{\text{T}}\bm{w}^{l}_{\text{out}}a_{l}(1-a_{l}){\bm{V}}}}}\right\rangle_{{\bm{Z}}{},{\bm{\hat{U}}}{},{\bm{V}}{}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We refer to the descent of (<a href="#S4.E11" title="In IV-A Approximating the BSFF gradient ‣ IV Methods ‣ Energy-Efficient Supervised Learning with aBinary Stochastic Forward-Forward Algorithm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) as <span id="S4.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">binary stochastic forward-forward (BSFF)</span>, and consider it to be the main contribution of this study.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Still, from the perspective of hardware acceleration, (<a href="#S4.E11" title="In IV-A Approximating the BSFF gradient ‣ IV Methods ‣ Energy-Efficient Supervised Learning with aBinary Stochastic Forward-Forward Algorithm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) has one flaw: the factor <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{l}(1-a_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{l}(1-a_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> is a real number.
Observe that this term corresponds to a smooth hill of activity centered on 0 and monotonically decreasing to zero away from 0 (blue curve, Fig. <a href="#S4.F2.sf1" title="In Fig. 2 ‣ IV-A Approximating the BSFF gradient ‣ IV Methods ‣ Energy-Efficient Supervised Learning with aBinary Stochastic Forward-Forward Algorithm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2a</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We can approximate that hill with the following function:</p>
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left[a_{l}\leq\frac{1}{2}}}\right]\hat{u}_{l}+\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left[a_{l}&gt;\frac{1}{2}}}\right](1-\hat{u}_{l})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left[a_{l}\leq\frac{1}{2}}}\right]\hat{u}_{l}+\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left[a_{l}&gt;\frac{1}{2}}}\right](1-\hat{u}_{l}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[P]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[P]</annotation></semantics></math> equal to 1 if <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is true, otherwise 0 (that is, an Iverson bracket).
Unlike <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{l}(1-a_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{l}(1-a_{l})</annotation></semantics></math>, this quantity is integer-valued.
Nevertheless, its expected value as a function of the input to the unit,</p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left[a_{l}\leq\frac{1}{2}}}\right]a_{l}+\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left[a_{l}&gt;\frac{1}{2}}}\right](1-a_{l})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left[a_{l}\leq\frac{1}{2}}}\right]a_{l}+\mathopen{}\mathclose{{\left[a_{l}&gt;\frac{1}{2}}}\right](1-a_{l}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">shown in green in Fig. <a href="#S4.F2.sf1" title="In Fig. 2 ‣ IV-A Approximating the BSFF gradient ‣ IV Methods ‣ Energy-Efficient Supervised Learning with aBinary Stochastic Forward-Forward Algorithm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2a</span></a>, is close to <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{l}(1-a_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{l}(1-a_{l})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Intuitively, (<a href="#S4.E12" title="In IV-A Approximating the BSFF gradient ‣ IV Methods ‣ Energy-Efficient Supervised Learning with aBinary Stochastic Forward-Forward Algorithm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) reports “surprising” events:
It is 1 when either unit <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> “spiked” despite its probability of spiking being below 1/2; or when this unit did <em id="S4.SS1.p3.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> spike, despite having a probability of spiking <em id="S4.SS1.p3.3.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">above</em> 0.5.
Otherwise, the function is 0.
Such “unexpected” pairings clearly become more common as the probability of spiking approaches 1/2 from either side, or equivalently, as the input (including the bias) approaches zero.
Thus the expected value of (<a href="#S4.E12" title="In IV-A Approximating the BSFF gradient ‣ IV Methods ‣ Energy-Efficient Supervised Learning with aBinary Stochastic Forward-Forward Algorithm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) is, like <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{l}(1-a_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{l}(1-a_{l})</annotation></semantics></math>, a hill of activity peaking at zero input.</p>
</div>
